1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,256
I'm fine...

2
00:00:03,306 --> 00:00:07,450
Work's been... fine.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:19,911
For Vikram, a deaf psychology student,
every client sounds like this.

4
00:00:19,961 --> 00:00:24,450
The words arrive. How they were said
doesn't.

5
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:29,333
Vikram lip-reads and uses Indian Sign
Language.

6
00:00:29,383 --> 00:00:31,977
Captions can give him the words.

7
00:00:32,027 --> 00:00:38,091
But trainee psychologists are also taught
to notice how something is said:

8
00:00:38,141 --> 00:00:42,966
a pause before an answer, a voice that
goes quiet or tight.

9
00:00:43,016 --> 00:00:45,444
That channel is closed to him.

10
00:00:45,494 --> 00:00:52,384
His challenge asked for a tool that shows
vocal tone in real time, during supervised

11
00:00:52,434 --> 00:00:53,128
practice.

12
00:00:53,178 --> 00:00:54,450
So we built one.

13
00:00:55,500 --> 00:01:01,901
It shows five things about a voice: pitch,
loudness, pauses, pace and voice quality.

14
00:01:01,951 --> 00:01:06,126
It always compares a person with their own
usual voice,

15
00:01:06,176 --> 00:01:09,505
which it learns in the first twenty
seconds,

16
00:01:09,555 --> 00:01:13,422
so a naturally quiet or deep voice isn't a
finding.

17
00:01:13,472 --> 00:01:15,419
It never names an emotion.

18
00:01:15,469 --> 00:01:19,950
It shows the evidence, and the judgement
stays with Vikram.

19
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:47,572
Take A: the line stays around her usual
middle, white, with even syllable ticks.

20
00:01:47,622 --> 00:01:54,607
Take B: these shaded columns are pauses of
more than a second, darker the longer they

21
00:01:54,657 --> 00:01:55,021
last.

22
00:01:55,071 --> 00:01:58,580
The line sits higher and rises at the
ends.

23
00:01:58,630 --> 00:02:02,636
Take C: a thick, bright line, which means
louder.

24
00:02:02,686 --> 00:02:08,678
The ticks are packed together and the pace
bar is up, which means faster.

25
00:02:08,728 --> 00:02:12,982
And amber: her voice is more pressed than
her usual.

26
00:02:13,032 --> 00:02:19,107
Take D: thin, dim and hugging the middle,
which means quieter and flatter.

27
00:02:19,157 --> 00:02:21,673
And blue: softer and breathier.

28
00:02:21,723 --> 00:02:24,487
Same words, four different voices.

29
00:02:24,537 --> 00:02:29,950
The app doesn't say what any of them
means. It shows what changed.

30
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,659
Afterwards, the supervisor and Vikram look
back together.

31
00:03:40,709 --> 00:03:45,367
The app lists where the voice stayed away
from its usual:

32
00:03:45,417 --> 00:03:51,893
here, a long pause and a quieter, flatter
answer, just after the question about

33
00:03:51,943 --> 00:03:52,307
home.

34
00:03:52,357 --> 00:03:55,693
It describes the voice, never the person.

35
00:03:55,743 --> 00:04:00,733
Deciding what that means, and whether to
gently ask about it,

36
00:04:00,783 --> 00:04:04,450
is Vikram's job, the same as for any
trainee.

37
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:10,027
It's made to be read from the corner of
the eye while lip-reading:

38
00:04:10,077 --> 00:04:15,435
shape, thickness, brightness and colour,
with no text and no numbers while you

39
00:04:15,485 --> 00:04:15,851
watch.

40
00:04:15,901 --> 00:04:19,180
Every rule is 'more is up, thicker or
brighter'.

41
00:04:19,230 --> 00:04:24,450
Blue and amber stay distinct for colour-
blind viewers, and nothing flashes.

42
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:30,692
It has limits, and we show them. Two
people talking at once get mixed together.

43
00:04:30,742 --> 00:04:34,275
A voice through a small speaker looks
falsely pressed.

44
00:04:34,325 --> 00:04:36,265
Whispers have no pitch at all.

45
00:04:36,315 --> 00:04:39,450
Knowing where it fails is part of using it
well.

46
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:44,685
It runs on an ordinary laptop, offline,
and saves no sound.

47
00:04:44,735 --> 00:04:50,356
Next, in Stage 2, we want to test it with
Vikram in real supervised role-plays,

48
00:04:50,406 --> 00:04:54,950
and add a second microphone so it shows
only the client's voice.

